.TH PSF\-SECURITY\-PATCHES 7 "30 October 2026" "psf-security-patches 26.10.30-1.0.0" "Miscellaneous"
.ad l
.SH NAME
.ad l
psf\-security\-patches \- security checks, hunts and protection for serious vulnerabilities
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ad l
.PP
Free security tools for GNU/Linux servers (and one for developer machines), written by PrivacySafe Foundation with the help of Ivy Cyber and released into the public domain. When a serious vulnerability is announced, each tool tells you whether your machine is exposed and looks for signs that someone has already used the flaw. Where there is a safe way to do it, the tool also protects the machine until you can update. Everything is plain bash.
.SH GETTING STARTED
.ad l
.IP 1. 3
Run the tool's \fBcheck\fR. It changes nothing.
.IP 2. 3
If you are exposed, install the vendor's update. That is the permanent fix.
.IP 3. 3
If you cannot update yet, run \fBmitigate \-\-dry\-run\fR to see what the protection would do, then \fBmitigate\fR. \fBrollback\fR undoes it.
.IP 4. 3
If the flaw was being exploited, run \fBhunt\fR, then change any password, key or token an attacker could have reached.
